## Measurement stance (STANDING-ORDERS §7)

  The subject is whether a specific frame is forwarded and **translated to a link-layer
  broadcast on egress**. That cannot be seen from a ping or from `show running-config`: it
  is visible only in the Ethernet destination of the egress frame. Every leg crafts the
  frame with `tools/arpleg.py` from host eth3 and decodes what arrives on host eth4,
  including both MACs.

  The crafted frame is the correct shape for a directed broadcast: a **unicast at layer 2**
  to the DUT's SVI MAC, carrying an IP destination of 10.38.22.95 -- the subnet broadcast
  of the far subnet -- from a source in a different subnet. The DUT must route it and only
  then flood it.

    ingress  vlan10  10.38.22.34/27  port1.0.10 <- host eth3.  Sources 10.38.22.40 / .42
    egress   vlan48  10.38.22.66/27  port1.0.48 <- host eth4.  Subnet broadcast 10.38.22.95

  **Control in EVERY counted leg:** a routed ICMP unicast 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65, which
  must always arrive on eth4. It arrived 5/5 in all seven legs below, so every zero in this
  case is a measurement and not a dead capture.

  🔴 **Foreign-host exposure, handled.** 10.38.22.41 / 00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 is on the INGRESS
  segment and is not ours. The counted side is eth4 / 10.38.22.64/27, which has no known
  foreign host, and the group's 30 s background baseline (T44181) measured 0 UDP and 0 ICMP
  there. Every frame is attributed by source address and by both MACs, and the denied-source
  address used below is 10.38.22.42 -- deliberately NOT .41, so no leg can be confused with
  the foreign host's own traffic.

