## LEG 1 (designed) — BASELINE OFF: the default is no directed broadcast

  No `ip directed-broadcast` anywhere (it is absent from the running-config by default).

    tools/tb sudotool tb277 ckt1406b arpleg --tag s1-baseline-off \
      --out /tmp/ckt1406b/T44182 --iface eth3 --watch eth4 \
      --udp 10.38.22.95:7777,10.38.22.95:7778 --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 \
      --icmp 10.38.22.65 --sender-ip 10.38.22.40 --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 \
      --count 5 --gap 0.3 --settle 3 --wait 8 --filter "udp or icmp"

    cap s1-baseline-off-eth3.pcap: 15 frame(s)
      x5  UDP  10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.95 :7777 [ours] [eth 00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
      x5  UDP  10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.95 :7778 [ours] [eth 00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours] [eth 00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
    cap s1-baseline-off-eth4.pcap: 5 frame(s)
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours] [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f]

  => All 10 directed broadcasts were delivered to the DUT (they are in the eth3 capture,
     addressed to its SVI MAC) and **none was forwarded**. The routed ICMP control arrived
     5/5 in the same capture, so the DUT was routing between the two subnets throughout.
  => Confirms the guide's default: directed broadcasts are dropped unless enabled.
  VERDICT leg 1: PASS (baseline OFF established before any positive claim).

## LEG 2 (designed) — the direction discriminator: enabled on the WRONG (ingress) interface

  The easiest way to get this feature wrong, and `show running-config` gives no hint:

    awplus(config)#interface vlan10
    awplus(config-if)#ip directed-broadcast
    awplus#show running-config interface vlan10
    interface vlan10
     ip address 10.38.22.34/27
     ip directed-broadcast                 <- present, and useless here
    awplus#show running-config interface vlan48
    interface vlan48
     ip address 10.38.22.66/27             <- nothing

    cap s2-wrong-if-eth4.pcap: 5 frame(s)
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours] [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f]

  => **Still 0/10 forwarded.** The command on the INGRESS interface does nothing. It is the
     interface attached to the DESTINATION subnet that matters, exactly as the guide says.
     Recorded as O-19 below, because a configuration that looks enabled can be completely
     inert.
  VERDICT leg 2: PASS (the feature is correctly scoped to the egress interface).

## LEG 3 (designed) — enabled on the DESTINATION subnet's interface

    awplus(config)#interface vlan10
    awplus(config-if)#no ip directed-broadcast
    awplus(config)#interface vlan48
    awplus(config-if)#ip directed-broadcast

    cap s3-right-if-eth4.pcap: 15 frame(s)
      x5  UDP  10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.95 :7777 [ours] [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
      x5  UDP  10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.95 :7778 [ours] [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours] [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f]

  => 10/10 forwarded, and -- the point of the case -- **translated to a link-layer
     broadcast**: the egress Ethernet destination is `ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff` while the IP
     destination stays 10.38.22.95. The IP source (10.38.22.40) and both UDP ports are
     unchanged. Byte-for-byte the same frames that legs 1 and 2 dropped.
  => "flooded as a broadcast on the destination subnet" is confirmed in the Ethernet
     header, which is the only place it is visible.
  VERDICT leg 3: PASS.

