      x5  UDP  10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.95 :7777 [ours]
      x5  UDP  10.38.22.42 -> 10.38.22.95 :7778 [ours]
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours]
    cap s4-acl-eth4.pcap: 10 frame(s)
      x5  UDP  10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.95 :7777 [ours] [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours] [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f]

  => **Permitted source 5/5 forwarded as a broadcast; denied source 0/5** -- in one
     capture, with the control also present. The ACL filters on the SOURCE address and the
     implicit deny applies to everything not permitted.
  VERDICT leg 4: PASS.

## LEG 5 (designed) — invert the ACL: is it consulted per packet, or merely present?

  A permitted-vs-denied pair alone cannot tell "the ACL is enforced" from "this particular
  source happens to work". Inverting the permit answers it:

    awplus(config)#no access-list 1
    awplus(config)#access-list 1 permit 10.38.22.42 0.0.0.0
    awplus#show access-list 1 ->  4 permit 10.38.22.42

    cap s5-acl-inverted-eth4.pcap: 10 frame(s)
      x5  UDP  10.38.22.42 -> 10.38.22.95 :7778 [ours] [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 [ours] [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f]

  => The exact MIRROR of leg 4: .42 now passes 5/5 and .40 is 0/5, with nothing else
     changed. The ACL is consulted per packet and the match really is on the source
     address.
  VERDICT leg 5: PASS.

