## Findings and observations

  **No F-level defect.** Every claim in the guide's IP Directed Broadcast section was
  confirmed in the dataplane, including the one that is only visible in the Ethernet header.

  🟠 O-19 — `ip directed-broadcast` configured on the INGRESS interface is completely
     inert, and nothing in `show running-config` says so. Measured: byte-identical frames
     to 10.38.22.95 were dropped 0/10 with the command on vlan10 and forwarded 10/10 with
     it on vlan48, with no other change. The guide does say "the interface via which the
     switch connects to that destination subnet", but this is the single easiest thing to
     get wrong in this feature, a wrong configuration reads as a product failure, and
     there is no warning and no `show` command that reveals it. Worth a note in the
     product documentation and worth carrying into any future directed-broadcast case.
     Evidence: work/s2-wrong-if.log vs work/s3-right-if.log.

  Measured platform facts worth keeping (x960 §7 material):
   - the egress frame keeps the subnet-broadcast IP destination (10.38.22.95) and its
     original IP source and UDP ports; only the Ethernet destination changes, to
     `ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff`. A tester looking for a rewritten IP destination will not find one;
   - `ip directed-broadcast [<1-99>|<1300-1999>|WORD]` filters on the packet's SOURCE
     address, is consulted per packet (proven by inverting the permit), and the standard
     ACL's implicit deny applies;
   - `no ip directed-broadcast` removes the ACL-bound form `ip directed-broadcast 1`
     without needing the argument -- the negation need not mirror the positive form.

## Run quality

  CLEAN. No confounder. Baseline OFF before any positive claim; the SAME frames dropped
  and then forwarded, so nothing rests on comparing different stimuli; a routed unicast
  control in all seven counted legs; the counted side on the segment with no foreign host;
  the ACL limb carried permitted and denied sources in ONE capture and was then inverted;
  removal and reboot persistence both proven in the dataplane; teardown diff empty. The one
  moment the instrument was in doubt -- STP at `Learning` right after the reload -- was
  resolved with a confirming read rather than ignored.

VERDICT: PASS -- no authored step text exists, so the case was designed from the IP Feature
Overview Guide (C613-22007-00 REV I p.17) and the DUT's CLI surface. Directed broadcast is
off by default and the frames are dropped; enabled on the destination subnet's interface
the identical frames are forwarded and translated to a link-layer broadcast; enabled on the
ingress interface it is inert (O-19); the optional standard ACL filters on source and is
consulted per packet, proven by inverting it; and removal and reboot persistence are both
proven in the dataplane. No platform defect; one observation (O-19).
